### Manual walkthrough of the session API (Days 15-16: cookie auth, CSRF, session revalidation, rate limiting).
### Requires: `docker compose up -d`, the API running on its "https" launch profile (the session and antiforgery
### cookies are Secure + __Host-, which a real browser — and the antiforgery system itself — only accepts over
### HTTPS), and staff seeded (`dotnet run --project src/TutoringCentre.Api -- seed`, with Seed:Password configured):
###   dotnet dev-certs https --trust   (once per machine)
###   dotnet run --project src/TutoringCentre.Api --launch-profile https
###
### The password is never written here. Create a git-ignored `.env` file at the repo root containing:
###   password=<the Seed:Password value you configured>
### VS Code's REST Client extension reads it via {{$dotenv password}}; no secret is ever committed.

@baseUrl = https://localhost:7197

### 0. Fetch a CSRF token — expect 200, Cache-Control: no-store, and the __Host-tcm.xsrf cookie.
### Every POST below sends it back as X-XSRF-TOKEN; re-run this step whenever a POST fails with 403 auth.csrf_invalid.
# @name antiforgery
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/antiforgery

### 1. Log in as owner@nile.test — expect 200, Set-Cookie __Host-tcm.session, activeCentreId = nile-centre's id
# @name loginOwner
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "owner@nile.test",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

### 2. GET /api/me — expect 200, active centre nile-centre
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 3. Log in as teacher@both.test — expect 200, no active centre (two active memberships, no auto-select)
# @name loginTeacher
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "teacher@both.test",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

### 4. GET /api/me — expect 200, activeCentreId null, two memberships.
### Copy the "maadi-hub" membership's centreId from the response body into @maadiHubCentreId below.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 5. Select maadi-hub — expect 204
@maadiHubCentreId = replace-with-the-centreId-from-step-4
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/session/centre
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "centreId": "{{maadiHubCentreId}}"
}

### 6. GET /api/me — expect 200, active centre maadi-hub
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 7. Select a random GUID — expect 403 tenant.no_membership; /api/me still shows maadi-hub afterwards
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/session/centre
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "centreId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}

### 8. Logout WITHOUT the CSRF header — expect 403 auth.csrf_invalid; the session still works afterwards
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout

### 8b. GET /api/me — expect 200 (step 8 had no side effects)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### 9. Logout, this time with the token — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/logout
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgery.response.body.$.token}}

### 10. GET /api/me — expect 401
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/me

### Manual walkthrough of the subjects API (Day 26): the six Subject endpoints against a real session.

### 11. Fetch a fresh CSRF token (the session from step 9 ended) — expect 200
# @name antiforgerySubjects
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/antiforgery

### 12. Log in as owner@nile.test — expect 200, active centre nile-centre
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "owner@nile.test",
  "password": "{{$dotenv password}}"
}

### 13. List subjects — expect 200, { "items": [ { "id", "name", "status", "version" } ] } for Nile only
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects

### 14. Create a subject — expect 201, Location: /api/subjects/{newId}, body { "id" }
# @name createSubject
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "Geography"
}

### 15. GET the created subject via the Location header — expect 200, name "Geography", status "active"
GET {{baseUrl}}{{createSubject.response.headers.Location}}

### 16. Rename it — copy the "id" from step 14 and the "version" from step 15 below first — expect 204
@subjectId = replace-with-the-id-from-step-14
@subjectVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-15
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects/{{subjectId}}
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "name": "World Geography",
  "version": {{subjectVersion}}
}

### 17. Archive it with the now-stale @subjectVersion from step 16 — expect 409 concurrency.stale.
### Re-run step 15 to read the current version, update @subjectVersion, then re-run this request — expect 204.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects/{{subjectId}}/archive
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{subjectVersion}}
}

### 18. Restore it — re-run step 15 first to refresh @subjectVersion to the archived row's current version — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/subjects/{{subjectId}}/restore
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{subjectVersion}}
}

### Manual walkthrough of the staff API and password change (Day 30), continuing owner@nile.test's session from step 12.

### 19. List staff — expect 200, { "items": [ { "membershipId", "userId", "displayName", "email", "role", "status", "version", "isCurrentUser" } ] }
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/staff

### 20. Create a staff member — expect 201, Cache-Control: no-store, body { "membershipId", "userId", "temporaryPassword" }
# @name createStaff
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/staff
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "email": "new-teacher@nile.test",
  "displayName": "New Teacher",
  "role": "teacher",
  "preferredLocale": "en"
}

### 21. Change their role — copy "membershipId" from step 20 and the new member's "version" from step 19 (re-run it) first — expect 204
@staffMembershipId = replace-with-the-membershipId-from-step-20
@staffVersion = replace-with-the-version-from-step-19-rerun
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/staff/{{staffMembershipId}}/role
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "role": "secretary",
  "version": {{staffVersion}}
}

### 22. Deactivate them — re-run step 19 first to refresh @staffVersion — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/staff/{{staffMembershipId}}/deactivate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{staffVersion}}
}

### 23. Reactivate them — re-run step 19 first to refresh @staffVersion to the deactivated row's current version — expect 204
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/staff/{{staffMembershipId}}/reactivate
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "version": {{staffVersion}}
}

### 24. Change the current user's own password — expect 204, Set-Cookie __Host-tcm.session with a new stamp and no
### centre selected. Use the created staff member's "temporaryPassword" from step 20 as the current password if
### you log in as them instead; here the owner changes their own seeded password.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/change-password
Content-Type: application/json
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{antiforgerySubjects.response.body.$.token}}

{
  "currentPassword": "{{$dotenv password}}",
  "newPassword": "a-new-password-that-is-long-enough"
}
